 <iati-activity default-currency="ZAR" hierarchy="1" humanitarian="0" iati-activities:version="2.03" xml:lang="EN" xmlns:iati-activities="http://d-portal.org/xmlns/iati-activities">
  <iati-identifier>ZA-NPO-189357-LA</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="ZA-NPO-189357" type="22">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sisonke National Sex Workers Movement</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Love Alliance Program</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">1.To share our area of Movement Building expertise with Love Alliance partners in all Love Alliance countries. 2.To capacitate key populations in advocacy, lobbying and leadership.3.To strengthen key population-led movements networks in Southern Africa.4.To hold decision-makers accountable by participating in national, regional and global spaces.5.To mobilise key populations to support one another in navigating legal processes and documenting human rights violations.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sisonke, SANPUD and GALZ have vast experience in movement building, advocacy for decriminalisation, SRHR (including HIV and harm reduction) programming in South Africa. The organisations will foster mutual learning amongst themselves and the other consortium and advocacy partners on intersecting issues, movement building and advocacy with organisations in West Africa, East Africa and the MENA region through partner exchange visits and learning events.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="4">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">We have developed a Movement Building Manual as a tool to capacitate Love Alliance partners on how to build movements. Themes of the manual include building yourself as an activist, building communities to strengthen movements, strategizing and communication. The manual will be available in all Love Alliance languages (English. French, Portuguese, Arabic) and will also be available on online platforms as a course.Sisonke participates in the South African National AIDS Council (SANAC) Civil Society Forum, as a fully recognised Sex Work Sector. In this platform, we have capacitated sex workers to take leadership positions in district, provincial and national levels. This ensures that sex work matters stay relevant in the Civil Society space and this also builds our advocacy for decriminalisation of sex work.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">We target diverse key populations, including sex workers, LGBTQI+ and people who use drugs, by mobilisation, capacity building and strengthening movements.We also use our advocacy to target law-makers, law enforcement, religious leaders and government departments such as Department of Health, Department of Justice, Department of Social Development.</narrative>
  </description>
