  <participating-org activity-id="ZA-NPO-189357-LA" ref="ZA-NPO-189357" role="4" type="22">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sisonke National Sex Workers Movement</narrative>
  </participating-org>
  <activity-status code="2"></activity-status>
  <activity-date iso-date="2021-01-01" type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The National Sex Work Plan for HIV, TB, and STIs (2019-2022) was launched in October 2021, signaling progress toward decriminalization, as evidenced by its frequent mention in the document. In 2021, South Africa&#x2019;s Department of Justice began reviewing the Sexual Offences Amendment Act, with Sisonke submitting recommendations advocating for decriminalization. Additionally, Sisonke contributed to the Hate Crime Bill to combat violence and discrimination against sex workers. Throughout the year, government and civil society held dialogues to address sex workers&#x2019; access to justice, healthcare, and human rights.  Sisonke strengthened its efforts by appointing new staff and collaborating with organizations representing key populations, such as people who use drugs, LGBTQI+ individuals, and PLHIV. Partnerships with groups like Show Me Your Number led to community radio shows on human rights and SRHR. During COVID-19, Sisonke worked with NAPWA to ensure sex workers received HIV treatment. The Decriminalisation Working Group is engaging with the Department of Justice to amend laws and draft a decriminalization bill, consulting sex workers nationwide.  Provincial structures have been formalized to enhance sex worker representation in decision-making. Sisonke organizes workshops on stigma, discrimination, and gender-based violence, empowering sex workers to document rights violations and access legal support. The Dignity Diversity Policing (DDP) training, conducted with partners like Triangle Project and SANPUD, educates law enforcement on reducing brutality against sex workers, LGBTQI+ individuals, and drug users.  Sisonke&#x2019;s annual national meeting brings together sex workers from all provinces to discuss challenges and progress toward decriminalization. Outreach programs link sex workers to healthcare, including ART and SRHR services, while monitoring rights violations. The KwaZulu-Natal adoption of the National Sex Work Plan, endorsed by government officials, marks a significant step. A multisectoral approach ensures advocacy efforts address intersecting stigmas faced by different key populations. Innovations like WhatsApp groups, online campaigns, and webinars have expanded engagement, fostering collaboration and faster dissemination of advocacy initiatives.  In 2022, Sisonke trained its ReActors and Human Rights Defenders in lobbying strategies, documentation of rights violations, and engaging local decision-makers, strengthening advocacy for law reform. These efforts extended to outreach, where beneficiaries were educated on their rights. As the coordinator of South African grantees, Sisonke hosted monthly meetings to foster collaboration and address advocacy gaps among key populations (KPs). Engagement with the Love Alliance Working Groups provided insights into global advocacy, while consultations on the Policy Scoping Review highlighted research gaps, such as insufficient data on transgender individuals and young people, as well as underfunding of community-led organizations. A major milestone was the Decriminalisation Symposium, which brought together grantees, KPs, and the public to discuss barriers to health and justice, reinforcing collective advocacy efforts.A significant national development was the launch of South Africa&#x2019;s Gender-Based Violence and Femicide (GBV-F) Plan, where President Ramaphosa declared GBV a &quot;second pandemic.&quot; Crucially, the plan explicitly calls for sex work decriminalization&#x2014;a major advocacy victory. Sisonke and civil society must now monitor implementation. Additionally, government dialogues, such as the Northern Cape engagement with the Department of Social Development, addressed GBV&#x2019;s impact on marginalized groups, with activists highlighting the urgent need for sex workers&#x2019; rights. In court cases, Sisonke mobilized support for victims, including assisting families in identifying murdered sex workers and ensuring proper legal processes. Partner organizations like Access Chapter 2 helped KPs understand court procedures, empowering them to demand justice.The Decriminalisation Working Group intensified lobbying efforts, consulting sex workers nationwide to shape the draft bill, emphasizing equal access to justice and healthcare. South African Love Alliance grantees developed a unified advocacy strategy focusing on law reform, drug policy, media accountability, and inclusive education. Lobbying trainings equipped Human Rights Defenders and Peer Educators with skills to engage government structures, while photography workshops (in partnership with Market Photo Workshop) provided KPs a creative outlet to depict stigma and discrimination. A Decriminalisation Webinar for International Sex Workers Day examined global progress and the intersection of law reform and healthcare access.Sisonke conducted SRHR workshops in six provinces, documenting challenges in healthcare access, such as long queues and lack of mobile clinics. A critical issue was food insecurity affecting medication adherence, prompting plans to collaborate with SANCO and the Department of Social Development. The workshops reinforced sex workers&#x2019; rights to safe, satisfying sexual health, including access to contraception and STI prevention. These efforts, alongside partnerships with health organizations, aim to ensure equitable services for all KPs, advancing both health and human rights.In 2023, several foreseen challenges emerged - anti-key population (KP) movements demonstrated their influence, particularly during submissions on the bill to decriminalize sex work, where opposition outnumbered support. Despite this, we argue that numerical dominance does not reflect majority opinion. Criminalization of KPs remained a significant barrier, hindering access to sexual and reproductive health rights (SRHR), social services, and justice. Sex workers faced police harassment and violence, while regional criminalization forced migration, exposing migrant sex workers to further exploitation. Natural disasters, like KZN&#x2019;s floods, disrupted health services, highlighting poor implementation of disaster laws. Civil unrest and service delivery protests also increased, with sex workers often targeted in violent outbreaks. Despite challenges, opportunities arose in advocacy and movement building. Participation in SWEAT&#x2019;s Youth Summit allowed engagement with young political leaders on decriminalization, fostering future policy influence. The Department of Social Development remained a key ally, providing access to social programs for sex workers. The renewed National Strategic Plan on HIV, TB, and STIs (2023&#x2013;2028) recognized decriminalization as vital to global AIDS targets. We developed a Movement Building Manual to support youth-led KP movements, emphasizing solidarity, intersectionality, and activist well-being. Learning hubs in Southern Africa facilitated knowledge exchange, with plans to expand capacity-building for sex workers in Zimbabwe and Mozambique. These efforts empowered sex workers through leadership training, rights education, and advocacy skills, increasing their participation in policy discussions and improving working conditions.A 2023 survey and focus group discussions in the Western Cape gathered qualitative data on young sex workers&#x2019; SRHR challenges and rights violations. This research will inform a 2024 Leadership Training Manual to better address sex workers&apos; needs. Creative spaces also played a crucial role, with sex workers using storytelling and art to raise awareness on SRHR, gender-based violence (GBV), and human rights. These platforms combatted stigma, provided mental health support, and amplified marginalized voices. Court support initiatives, like mobilizing sex workers during the Sfiso Mkhwanazi serial killer case, strengthened justice system engagement and visibility through demonstrations and law enforcement sensitization.Regional police dialogues with SARPCCO (Southern Africa Regional Police Chiefs Coordinating Organisation) addressed systemic challenges faced by sex workers in South Africa, Mozambique, and Zimbabwe. Sensitization training improved police attitudes, fostering trust and safer interactions. Advocacy efforts also influenced policymaking, promoting decriminalization and better protections for sex workers. Adopting best practices in police training could reduce violence, improve access to justice, and advance decriminalization efforts&#x2014;though success depends on political will and resource allocation. Overall, 2023 highlighted both persistent barriers and progress in empowering sex workers through advocacy, research, and creative resistance.</narrative>
  </activity-date>
